today's classic interview is with Todd  Wolfson and it's stunning how in  retrospect it was so prescient at the  time  the interview was in January of 2015 and  the topic was whether there is a cyber  right and this now relates in 2018 to  Russian hacking it relates to memo gate  as we spoke about earlier in the week  with Molly McHugh it relates to the alt  rights use of the Internet it relates to  fake news at the time when we spoke to  Todd Wolfson a lot of those developments  hadn't yet taken place so let's get to  this week's classic interview with Todd  Wolf's wolfson about the potential of a  cyber right from a few years ago I'm  joined today by Todd Wolfson who's an  assistant professor at Rutgers  University co-founder of the media  mobilizing project and also author of  the book digital rebellion the birth of  the cyber left this is a really  interesting topic Todd and I'm curious  to start with do you think that there is  a sort of cyber right or is there  something about the internet that is  inherently conducive to being used for  more progressive causes that's a  fantastic question and it's really great  to have a chance to talk to you David I  do think there's something conducive to  a progressive movement and the internet  at least I mean with with some with a  lot of caveats but but the the  conduciveness is that the Internet as it  was developed has and as many people  mark has a democratic participatory  nature which plays very well to both  traditional progressive positions and  certainly as we see with things like  General Assemblies in the Occupy  movement very much show of parallelism  to the contemporary left or what I'm  calling a cyber left so there is a cyber  right I don't think it is developed and  I think this emerges out of a particular  logic which is much more conducive to a  certain form of life based organizing  and sometimes we see that come through  in particular campaigns I mean I  remember during the 2008 presidential  campaign John McCain's campaigns use of  the internet was just a disaster and as  someone who was kind of engaged with  both campaigns as a journalist to see  how they each used the the tools the  internet provided there was just no  comparison at all to the way that that  President Obama was using it what's your  thought though about the the kind of  anti net neutrality movement because  when we step back the Internet to me  seems inherently kind of like as you  said a democratic and even sort of  libertarian space so to speak and the  anti net neutrality movement seems to me  like it would it would fundamentally  change what the internet is obviously  new trial is is a critical policy battle  that we have in front of us it looks  things are looking better today than  they did it maybe six months ago with  the president being a strong proponent  and hopefully that impacting how the FCC  chair and the FCC itself acts on this so  I don't know well what what's gonna  happen obviously none of us do but I do  think that that having strong net  neutrality rules is critical to the sort  of left progressive movement that we've  seen emerge and and so hopefully it will  continue to and and I think you saw you  know in response to SOPA and PIPA you  know in response to certain sorts of  moves on the around technology policy  that you've seen a certain sort of  progressive movement respond to it in a  very powerful way so I think I think  what we've seen as I left a certain sort  of progressive movement develop around  that neutrality but a much broader  progressive movement that has a stake in  it you talk in your book about what you  described is getting sort of lost in  cyberspace and this is something that  I've considered a little bit when we  talk about groups like anonymous and  their activism or even a lot of what  goes on under the gamergate hashtag  online which is that sometimes online  activism if it exists only online can  get kind of disconnected from the real  world so to speak and the way in which  people interact  off-line talked about that a little bit  how have you observed that yeah I mean  this comes out of my research on the  indie media movement which you know  developed in the late nineties 99 and  was really strong in the mid 2000s and  and what I saw in that movement was a  desire and I would say this is based on  the class position of those who were  primarily in the lead of indie media at  least in the u.s. is which which I mean  to say they were largely middle-class  white college-educated they had  technological proficiency amongst other  things maybe they had journalistic  backgrounds maybe they had organizing an  activist backgrounds but they tended to  see technology as the solution so  instead of taking technology and  embedding it in local struggles they  really worked hard to create really  important technological tools so the  indie media websites of 1999 through the  early 2000s I would argue preciate your  Facebook prefigure Twitter prefigure the  blogging movement and in fact one of the  programmers that was involved in the new  media was one of the programmers  involved in the early stages of Twitter  so the technology and what they figured  out there is quite impressive but I  would argue it comes at the expense of  being embedded in strong local  communities the best independent media  centers are developed in that movement  were ones that were embedded in the  community and those are few and far  between and I think that's a broader  emergent logic of contemporary activism  it's a problem right  and and so one that we need to struggle  against and one that we need to find  corrective corrective measures for do  you think that there are some venues and  maybe the Internet is one of them that  simply doesn't lend itself as well to  conservative ideology and I'll give you  a couple of examples sure in the midst  of the success of the daily show and I  think maybe it predated The Colbert  Report but but maybe around the same  time Fox News wanted to do a  conservative half-hour satirical news  show and they called it the half hour  news hour and it was a disaster I mean  it was it was just a complete disaster  and that started making people think  maybe  there's something about this type of  satire that just doesn't work with  conservative ideology is internet  activism something that you would  consider similar to that in other words  is there's something about the Internet  and conservative ideology that will not  work together or has the right simply  not figured it out yet I would say that  there I would say there's no absolutes  here there are yeah I think that the  Internet is more conducive to a to a  broadly understood progressive politic  but I do think that the right as the  internet changes as it becomes more  corporatized as the spaces become as we  go back to the net neutrality ruling as  these space has become more dominated by  certain interests I do think you're  gonna see development of very strong  right trajectory based movements through  the internet and but I think what you're  arguing is very very interesting yes it  you know something like The Daily Show  is something that plays very well to  progressive politic and then on the  Congress something like am talk radio  tends to play more strongly to a  conservative politic and and I think  that there's a logic and set of  practices problems and and communities  of interest that these movements or  these programs speak to that make them  more conducive to a certain political  vision I'll remind the audience the book  is digital rebellion the birth of the  cyber left we're speaking with the  author Todd Wolfson one of the last  things I want to talk about with you  Todd and this is kind of a big thing is  you talk in the book about horizontal  ISM and this idea that often these  progressive online movements and  sometimes you know even occupy which was  not an online movement have a lack of a  specific leader now some tout that lack  of a specific leader as a good thing  it's democratic it doesn't allow  individuals to to kind of sabotage or or  hold hostage the mission for their own  personal gain  but at the same time it has led to some  problems as well where anybody can sort  of wrap themselves with the hashtag or  the name of the movement and distract or  distort what the message is and it can  it can often lead to lack of clear  how can we suss out whether the the fact  that the Internet provides a platform  for leaderless movements is that a  positive or negative thing net yeah I  would say it tracks these days a little  bit negative  I think obviously this is something to  be held in balance when we look at  Occupy or in the media to very  horizontal formations what we see that's  so important about what they do is that  the horizontal ISM the self-organizing  aspect the lack of a centralized  structure allows for great growth both  in the media and occupy grew and  developed very quickly and powerfully  what they a couple things that they  don't do one they don't they tend to  again favor the leadership of people  with the most social and cultural  capital so when you think about indie  media or even occupy what you don't see  is people who have the who are the most  oppressed people of color poor people  immigrants it's very difficult for them  to take the same sort of leadership that  white college-educated males generally  tend to be able to take because the the  invisible structures of power that  exists in these sorts of formations tend  to dominate in those favored people who  have more social and cultural capital so  an imbalance I think that's a problem so  I think there are good things about it  it lets us build a sense of identity  with the movement when when it's deeply  democratic but we have to think about  building leaders actually I would argue  in these women so we may say we want to  build a thousand leaders but leader  listen intense to me what makes sense is  working with communities that are  oppressed and creating political  education programs so that we build a  clarity connectedness competency and  vision of the kind of world we want to  create and horizontality doesn't lend  itself to those sorts of politics  generally we've been speaking with  professor Todd Wolfson the book is  digital rebellion the birth of the cyber  left very interesting and timely topic  Todd thank you so much for being with us  thanks for having me David I really  appreciate it

